      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The need to correct outcome data for case mix is well recognized, but risk assessment for coronary care unit (CCU) patients remains problematic."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "HYPOTHESIS",
          "content": "This study determined the feasibility of using physicians' opinions to predict mortality for CCU patients and compared their results to Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation II (APACHE II) scores."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A prospective observational study was performed on consecutive patients admitted to a university-affiliated Veterans Affairs Medical Center CCU over a 2-month period. Physician assessment of likely mortality during hospitalization, obtained using an MD Prognosis Score ranging from 1 (best) to 7 (worst), was compared with APACHE II scores."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "MD Prognosis Scores were obtained on 122 of the 237 eligible patients (51% response rate) and averaged 2.3 +/- 1.4 (mean +/- standard deviation). APACHE II scores on these patients averaged 9.9 +/- 4.8 (range 2-29) with very poor correlation between the two methods (r = 0.3). Of the four patients who died, three had MD prognosis scores of 7. None of the survivors had scores of 7 and only three had scores of 6. APACHE II did not predict a high likelihood that any of the patients would die (none with > 90% likelihood of mortality)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "APACHE scores are inadequate for cardiac patients. Although physicians can identify CCU patients most likely to die, reliance on physician scoring systems is limited by difficulties in obtaining their opinion. A new method of risk assessment for acutely ill cardiac patients is needed if CCU outcomes are to be compared across institutions."
        }
      ]},
